INSERT INTO topic_templates (id, name, instruction) VALUES
    (1, 'ESG Strategy', 'ScopeHigh-level review of materiality assessments, ESG strategy (KPIs, objectives, and goals), and strategy implementation process. Operational definitionThis category covers how the Target defines and executes ESG priorities: material topics, targets/KPIs, roadmaps, governance, and evidence that ESG is embedded into decision-making (budgeting, risk, procurement, operations). In a European reporting context, materiality is often framed through a double materiality lens under ESRS. [2] Categorisation instructions Primary routing keywords (8–12): ESG strategy, sustainability strategy, materiality assessment, double materiality, ESG roadmap, sustainability goals, ESG KPIs, baseline year, targets, SDGs, programme governance, implementation plan. Metadata signals: folders “Strategy”, “ESG”, “Sustainability”, “Materiality”, “Roadmap”, “Board”; file names containing “strategy”, “materiality”, “ESG goals”, “KPI library”, “sustainability plan”. Types of documents expected in this topic: ESG strategy decks; materiality assessments; ESG KPI catalogues/scorecards; sustainability programme plans; ESG investment/business case memos; board updates on ESG. Examples of document titles: “ESG Strategy 2025–2028”; “Double Materiality Assessment”; “Sustainability KPI Definitions & Baselines”; “ESG Roadmap and Owners”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Sustainability report → usually better treated as Disclosure & Reporting primary, with ESG Strategy secondary if it includes strategy pillars/targets/roadmap.- A single KPI spreadsheet without narrative → tag ESG Strategy only if it is explicitly the strategy KPI set; otherwise tag the KPI’s underlying topic (Energy/Carbon/DEI/Supply Chain, etc.). Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: at least one of (materiality output; ESG strategy deck; KPI/targets list with owners).Strong evidence: strategy linked to material topics; baselines and time-bound targets; roadmap with owners and resourcing; evidence of tracking and internal reporting cadence. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): material topics and selection logic; ESG goals and KPI set; targets (baseline year + time horizon); roadmap (milestones, owners); evidence of implementation (initiatives underway); gaps (missing baselines/owners). Brief Finding Description (b): describe whether (i) materiality is documented, (ii) strategy priorities are clearly translated into measurable targets/KPIs, (iii) execution is governed (owners/cadence), and (iv) tracking data exists. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: close governance gaps if strategy exists but is not operationalised (no owners/cadence/no baselines).- Value Creation / Best-Practice: strengthen KPI coverage; align strategy to customer expectations and likely future reporting/due diligence demands. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if the Target makes external ESG claims without credible internal strategy/controls (reputational and misstatement risk).- Remediation effort Medium–High if the Target lacks materiality, target-setting, and management reporting systems entirely. Red flags examples: “Net zero” claims without baseline or boundary; KPI dashboard with missing site coverage; no linkage between stated priorities and operating initiatives. Open questions/data requests: materiality methodology and stakeholder inputs; KPI definitions/baselines; ESG governance cadence; budgeted programme plan; evidence of performance tracking. Sample filled example (end-report-aligned style)Brief Finding Description: The Target describes an ESG strategy informed by materiality and translated into a set of ESG priorities and KPIs. Targets are stated for selected topics; however, KPI definitions, baselines, and ownership are not consistently documented across the group, limiting confidence in progress tracking. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Refresh ESG goals and KPI definitions (including baselines and owners) to ensure tracking reflects the full operating footprint and supports credible external reporting readiness.'),
    (2, 'Organisation and Responsibility', 'ScopeHigh-level review of evidence of senior management engagement, oversight and available responsibilities for ESG issues. Operational definitionThis category covers who owns ESG: board/senior oversight, roles (e.g., Sustainability Manager), committee structures, RACI, site responsibilities (especially for EHS), and escalation/reporting lines. Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): ESG governance, oversight, organisation chart, roles and responsibilities, sustainability manager, ESG committee, board oversight, RACI, terms of reference, accountability, escalation, management review. Metadata signals: folders “Governance”, “Board”, “Committees”, “Organisation”, “Sustainability”; filenames “RACI”, “TOR”, “governance”. Types of documents expected in this topic: governance frameworks; org charts; committee charters; role descriptions; management review minutes; ESG steering committee packs.Examples of document titles: “ESG Governance Framework”; “Sustainability Team Org Chart”; “Board ESG Oversight Terms of Reference”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Site-level safety/environment role descriptions may also sit under EHS categories; keep this category focused on overall governance/ownership, not operational procedures. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: org chart or named ESG role(s), plus at least one governance artefact (meeting cadence, TOR, responsibilities list).Strong evidence: board-level oversight evidence; clear accountability across subsidiaries/sites; escalation channels; funding/resourcing. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): named ESG roles; governance structure; oversight cadence (board/committee); accountability at site/entity level; documented responsibilities and escalation. Brief Finding Description (b): describe whether ESG responsibilities are clearly assigned and whether senior oversight is evidenced. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: clarify ownership where gaps exist (especially for multi-site EHS and value chain risk).- Value Creation / Best-Practice: formalise governance cadence and reporting to enable consistent performance management. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if there is no clear accountable owner for material EHS/value-chain risks (increases execution and compliance risk).- Effort Low–Medium if roles exist but documentation and cadence are weak. Red flags examples: no ESG owner; reliance on informal “champions” only; subsidiaries not covered by group governance. Open questions/data requests: ESG governance org chart; committee minutes; role descriptions; site-level responsibility mapping; escalation procedures. Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: ESG ownership is assigned to a dedicated sustainability role, and responsibilities are documented at a high level. Evidence of consistent site-level accountability and senior oversight cadence is limited in the materials reviewed. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Clarify site/subsidiary accountability and formalise senior oversight cadence to support consistent implementation.'),
    (3, 'Policies and Business Ethics', 'ScopeReview of available policies (e.g., Code of Conduct) relating to business ethics (corruption, bribery, money laundering, etc.). Operational definitionThis category covers the Target’s ethics and compliance policy landscape and implementation: code(s) of conduct, anti-bribery/anti-corruption, conflicts, gifts/hospitality, sanctions controls, training, breach reporting, and supplier code alignment. Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): code of conduct, business ethics, anti-bribery, anti-corruption, gifts and hospitality, conflict of interest, sanctions, AML, compliance training, disciplinary action, supplier code of conduct, breach register. Metadata signals: folders “Compliance”, “Legal”, “Ethics”, “Policies”; filenames “ABAC”, “Code of Conduct”, “Sanctions”. Types of documents expected: internal code of conduct; ABAC/AML policies; training completion logs; supplier code; sanctions screening procedures; incident/breach registers; policy acknowledgement records. Examples of document titles: “Code of Conduct”; “Anti-Bribery & Corruption Policy”; “Gifts & Hospitality Register”; “Supplier Code of Conduct”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Whistleblowing policy → treat primarily under Grievance Mechanisms and Monitoring, unless it is embedded as part of a broader ethics code pack.- Supplier code and auditing governance may overlap with Supply Chain Management; keep this category focused on ethics frameworks and controls, not supplier monitoring mechanics. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: at least one ethics policy + evidence of rollout (distribution, acknowledgement, training, or governance). Strong evidence: complete policy suite + training evidence + breach monitoring (or explicit “no breaches” with evidence of monitoring mechanism). Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): policy set completeness; scope applicability (subsidiaries, contractors); training evidence; sanctions screening approach; breach/disciplinary evidence; code coverage (employee vs supplier). Brief Finding Description (b): describe (i) which policies exist, (ii) whether there is an internal code covering employees, (iii) whether implementation is evidenced (training/acknowledgement), and (iv) whether breaches are monitored. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: close policy gaps (e.g., missing employee code); ensure sanctions/ABAC controls fit the Target’s footprint.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: harmonise policies across subsidiaries and implement acknowledgement cycles. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if operating in higher-risk jurisdictions/contexts without robust ABAC/sanctions controls.- Effort Low–Medium if policies exist but rollout evidence is weak. Red flags examples: no internal code of conduct; no ABAC training evidence; inconsistent subsidiary policy adoption; supplier code excludes key topics. Open questions/data requests: policy inventory with dates; training completion statistics; acknowledgement logs; breach/disciplinary register; sanctions procedure evidence. Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: Multiple ESG-related policies are in place, including anti-bribery and a supplier code; however, an internal employee-facing code of conduct is not evidenced in the reviewed materials, and supplier code scope does not clearly cover employees. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Create/refresh an employee code of conduct and ensure group policies are implemented consistently across all operating entities.'),
    (4, 'Disclosure and Reporting', 'ScopeOverview of internal and external ESG disclosures, including reporting requirements (e.g., CSRD). Operational definitionThis category covers ESG reporting (sustainability reports, annual report ESG sections), reporting readiness (process, evidence trails), and scope assessment against applicable regimes. In the EU, CSRD reporting is linked to ESRS adopted via delegated acts. [3] Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): sustainability report, ESG report, CSRD, ESRS, EU taxonomy, disclosure, reporting scope, assurance, gap analysis, stakeholder reporting, data readiness, delegated act. Metadata signals: folders “Reporting”, “Annual Report”, “CSRD”, “ESRS”; filenames “sustainability report”, “ESRS mapping”, “taxonomy”. Types of documents expected: sustainability reports; CSRD/ESRS scoping memos; ESRS mapping tables; assurance statements; ESG metrics workbooks prepared for publication; regulatory gap analyses. Examples of document titles: “Sustainability Report FY2024”; “CSRD Scope Assessment”; “ESRS Gap Analysis”; “EU Taxonomy Eligibility Assessment”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- A sustainability report contains data across all topics; treat this as Disclosure & Reporting primary, with secondary tags for topics that have substantive sections.- Internal KPI dashboards not used for external reporting → tag the underlying content category instead. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: at least one published disclosure or internal reporting pack, with evidence of reporting governance (authors/owners/cadence). Strong evidence: documented reporting boundary; framework alignment; evidence trail for key KPIs; readiness/gap assessment; plan for assurance. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): what is reported (topics/KPIs); framework references; reporting boundary; data ownership and controls; assurance/verification; known gaps and roadmap. Brief Finding Description (b): describe whether (i) disclosure exists and is consistent, (ii) reporting governance/controls are credible, and (iii) reporting requirements are monitored. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: monitor reporting scope and maintain readiness (particularly if the Target may become in-scope based on growth/structure).- Value Creation / Best-Practice: align voluntary reporting structure to ESRS principles for comparability and investor/customer confidence.Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if disclosures are materially inconsistent with internal evidence (misstatement risk).- Effort Medium if building disclosure readiness requires new data controls and consolidation across entities. Red flags examples: contradictory KPI figures across reports; missing boundaries; “assured” claims without assurance statement; lack of documentation for key metrics. Open questions/data requests: reporting boundary memo; KPI definitions and data traceability; assurance scope; regulatory scope analysis date; draft reporting roadmap. Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: The Target produces a sustainability report and references ESRS-linked topics; however, scope applicability and future threshold monitoring are not clearly documented, and reporting readiness depends on strengthening KPI traceability and governance. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: Maintain a documented scope assessment and readiness plan in case future reporting requirements apply.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Structure voluntary reporting in a CSRD/ESRS-like format to improve comparability and readiness. [4]'),
    (5, 'Risk Management & Assurance', 'ScopeReview of procedures regarding internal ESG risks, third-party assessments, audits and certifications (e.g., ISO), and mitigation measures. Operational definitionThis category covers how ESG risks and controls are identified, monitored, and assured: risk registers, internal audit, certifications (ISO 14001/45001/50001 etc.), third-party ESG assessments, corrective actions, and evidence of continuous improvement. ISO standards provide management system frameworks frequently used as DD evidence (environmental management, OH&S, energy). [5] Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): enterprise risk management, risk register, internal audit, assurance, certification, ISO 14001, ISO 45001, ISO 50001, third-party ESG assessment, corrective action, audit findings, control framework. Metadata signals: folders “Audit”, “Risk”, “Assurance”, “Certifications”; filenames “risk register”, “ISO certificate”, “audit findings”. Types of documents expected: ERM risk registers; internal audit plans; ISO certificates and audit summaries; corrective action trackers; third-party ESG rating outputs; management review minutes. Examples of document titles: “ISO 14001 Certificate”; “Internal Audit Plan”; “ESG Risk Register”; “Corrective Action Log”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Operational-level EMS/OHS manuals → belong in Environmental Management or Health & Safety Management (EHS section) if content is procedural and site-focused.- Certificate-only documents → keep here. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: one assurance artefact (audit plan/certification/third-party assessment) plus evidence of corrective action handling. Strong evidence: ESG risks explicitly integrated into ERM; corrective actions tracked to closure; certification scope covers the full operating footprint. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): assurance coverage and scope (which entity/sites); audit frequency and results; corrective action handling; existence of ERM integration; gaps (uncertified sites). Brief Finding Description (b): describe whether controls are systematically assessed and whether assurance scope matches the operating footprint. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: incorporate ESG risks into ERM if missing; strengthen corrective action closure discipline.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: expand certification where it materially improves control consistency and customer confidence. [6] Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if high-risk operations lack systematic controls/assurance and incidents are plausible.- Effort Medium if ERM integration and corrective action systems require redesign.Red flags examples: certifications limited to one entity while risk footprint is broader; no evidence of audit findings closure; no ESG risks in risk register.Open questions/data requests: audit plans and last audit reports; certification scopes; corrective action tracker; ERM register extracts; third-party assessment details. Sample filled example Brief Finding Description: The Target holds selected management system certifications and uses internal systems for incident handling and corrective action tracking; however, ESG risks are not clearly evidenced as integrated into enterprise risk management. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Incorporate ESG considerations into ERM and clarify assurance coverage across all operating entities.'),
    (6, 'Customer Engagement', 'ScopeHigh-level review of customer engagement including processes (audits, complaints procedure, satisfaction surveys, etc.). Operational definitionThis category covers how the Target measures and manages customer relationships: satisfaction surveys (e.g., NPS or equivalent), complaint handling, customer audits, and customer-driven ESG requirements (e.g., supplier questionnaires). Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): customer satisfaction, NPS, customer survey, complaint procedure, returns, claims handling, service levels, customer audit, customer feedback, customer retention, service quality, escalation.Metadata signals: folders “Commercial”, “Sales”, “Customer Service”; filenames “survey results”, “complaints”, “customer audit”.Types of documents expected: survey reports; complaints logs/SOPs; customer audit questionnaires; service KPI dashboards; customer ESG requirement packs.Examples of document titles: “Customer Satisfaction Survey FY2024”; “Customer Complaints SOP”; “Customer Audit Response – ESG”.Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Employee grievance mechanisms → keep under Grievance Mechanisms and Monitoring.- Product quality/safety compliance → typically Sustainable Products. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: satisfaction survey output or complaints SOP.Strong evidence: multi-year trends; segmentation (major customer segments); root-cause analysis and improvement actions. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): satisfaction trends; complaint themes; process controls; customer ESG requirements; improvement actions. Brief Finding Description (b): describe performance trend, whether customer feedback is systematically monitored, and whether drivers of change are understood. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: strengthen complaint controls if recurring issues create contractual/regulatory risk.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: deepen survey design to identify drivers and improve retention and differentiation. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if satisfaction/complaints signal systemic service failure with high churn risk or major customer exposure.- Effort Low–Medium for survey improvements; higher if service redesign is required. Red flags examples: declining satisfaction without action; high complaint volumes with weak escalation; customer audits identifying repeated non-conformances. Open questions/data requests: survey methodology and raw results; complaint logs and closure evidence; customer audit findings; top customer requirements. Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: Customer satisfaction is measured annually and trend data exists across key segments; however, survey design could be strengthened to better capture drivers behind a modest decline in results over time.Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Continue annual surveys and refine to diagnose drivers of change; link insights to a structured improvement roadmap.'),
    (7, 'Supply Chain Management', 'ScopeHigh-level review of the Company’s policies and management relating to suppliers, including specific requirements (e.g., EU conflict minerals regulation where applicable). Operational definitionThis category covers supplier selection, onboarding, risk assessment, sanitation/sanctions screening, audits/visits, corrective actions, and traceability—especially where the Target sources from higher-risk geographies or handles regulated materials. EU conflict minerals rules impose due diligence obligations on EU importers of certain minerals/metals (3TG) from conflict-affected and high-risk areas. [7] Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): supplier code of conduct, procurement due diligence, supplier audit, supplier onboarding, high-risk suppliers, traceability, corrective action plan, sanctions screening, supplier risk rating, factory visit, social audit, conflict minerals (3TG). Metadata signals: folders “Procurement”, “Suppliers”, “Sourcing”, “Audits”; filenames “supplier list”, “audit”, “CoC acknowledgement”. Types of documents expected: supplier code; supplier lists (by geography/spend); audit reports; onboarding procedures; sanctions compliance process; supplier scoring/risk matrices; corrective action logs; conflict minerals statements (if relevant). Examples of document titles: “Supplier Code of Conduct”; “Supplier Audit Summary 2023–2025”; “Supplier Risk Matrix”; “Sanctions Screening Procedure”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Supplier code and audits overlap with Labour/Human Rights; keep Supply Chain as primary when the evidence is supplier-facing processes and controls.- If the Target is an EU importer of relevant 3TG flows, ensure conflict minerals statements and due diligence artefacts are tagged here. [8] Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: supplier code + supplier selection/monitoring process OR supplier audit/visit evidence.Strong evidence: supplier audit database export (date/type/outcome); defined scope boundaries (own brand vs third-party brands); high-risk supplier coverage; re-acknowledgement cycle; corrective action follow-up evidence. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): supplier footprint (regions, high-risk exposure); supplier code coverage; audit scope and frequency; sanctions screening; corrective action practices; gaps (no database export; outdated acknowledgements). Brief Finding Description (b): describe whether supplier controls are risk-based, whether audits/monitoring are credible and within defined scope, and whether evidence recency supports assurance. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: strengthen oversight for higher-risk suppliers; ensure sanctions screening/traceability is documented and consistently applied.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: clarify audit scope and coverage metrics; implement re-acknowledgement and better evidence hygiene to strengthen customer trust. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if significant sourcing from higher-risk geographies exists without audit coverage, traceability, or corrective action evidence.- Effort Medium–High if building a supplier risk programme from scratch. Red flags examples: no audit database; outdated proof of supplier code sign-off; supplier scope not defined; sourcing from high-risk areas without due diligence artefacts. Open questions/data requests: supplier audit database export; list of high-risk suppliers by geography and spend; corrective action tracking records; sanctions screening logs; conflict minerals applicability assessment. [7] Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: Supplier selection and monitoring are described as risk-based, supported by a supplier code and sanctions requirements; however, supporting evidence (acknowledgements and audit coverage) is partly outdated and audit scope is not fully transparent from the materials reviewed. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Refresh acknowledgement records (including re-acknowledgement cycle) and improve audit scope transparency through a structured database export and coverage metrics.'),
    (8, 'Sustainable Products', 'ScopeReview of the Company’s responsible/sustainable products and procedures for product lifecycle management, product quality and safety, and relevant KPIs. Operational definitionThis category covers sustainable assortment strategy (what counts as “sustainable”), product-related controls that enable credible claims (certifications, recycled/renewable content thresholds), product footprint/LCA programmes, packaging compliance and waste reduction, and constraints arising from product safety/quality. Where packaging is material, EU packaging rules can materially affect product redesign, labelling and customer commitments. [9] Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): sustainable products, ecolabel, certified products, recycled content, renewable materials, product sustainability criteria, assortment development, product footprint, life cycle assessment (LCA), packaging compliance, circular economy, product quality and safety. Metadata signals: folders “Product”, “Quality”, “Sustainability”, “Packaging”; filenames “LCA”, “product footprint”, “eco-label”. Types of documents expected: sustainable product strategy; criteria for sustainability-labelled products; certification evidence (e.g., forestry schemes); packaging compliance assessments; LCA/footprint plans; product KPI reporting; quality/safety procedures. Examples of document titles: “Sustainable Product Criteria”; “Sustainable Assortment KPI Dashboard”; “Packaging Compliance Plan”; “Product Carbon Footprint Roadmap”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Site-level waste management compliance → treat under Environmental Management (EHS), not Sustainable Products.- Supplier audits on product sustainability claims → may also sit under Supply Chain, but keep this category for product-side criteria and portfolio management. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: definition or criteria for “sustainable products” + at least one KPI/reporting artefact.Strong evidence: third-party certification evidence; documented thresholds (recycled/renewable); credible claim substantiation; LCA/footprint roadmap; packaging compliance plan. [10] Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): sustainable product definition and eligibility rules; KPIs (share of sustainable products, growth trend); certifications; LCA/footprint plans; packaging compliance readiness; waste reduction measures related to product/packaging. Brief Finding Description (b): describe whether sustainable product claims are substantiated by criteria and evidence, and whether portfolio KPIs show progress. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: strengthen substantiation and packaging compliance plans where regulatory exposure exists. [9]- Value Creation / Best-Practice: prioritise hotspot categories for LCA/footprints; develop supplier data approach for product footprints. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if product sustainability claims are not substantiated (greenwashing/reputational risk) or packaging rules drive major redesign cost. [10]- Effort Medium if redesign and supplier data collection are needed across a large portfolio. Red flags examples: “sustainable” label without criteria; no evidence for recycled content claims; no packaging compliance monitoring; inconsistent product KPIs. Open questions/data requests: sustainable product criteria and governance; full KPI history; certification list and scope; packaging compliance status; LCA/footprint methodology and timeline. Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: The Target has a product sustainability strategy focused on increasing sustainable-labelled products and those with recycled/renewable content; KPIs show gradual year-on-year improvement, while product-level footprint and detailed waste splits are still being developed. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Prioritise product footprint work on hotspot categories and strengthen KPI traceability to support credible claims and future compliance.'),
    (9, 'Labour Laws & Human Rights', 'ScopeReview of policies and procedures implemented by the Company, and review of outstanding or pending labour law related litigations and claims. Operational definitionThis category covers labour law compliance risk and human rights exposure (own operations—and where relevant, value chain), including dispute history, dismissal processes, and alignment with widely accepted expectations for responsible business conduct. UNGP clarifies corporate responsibility to respect human rights and the role of remedy. [11] Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): labour law, human rights, modern slavery, forced labour, child labour, litigation, tribunal claim, settlement, wrongful dismissal, labour dispute, grievance (legal), disciplinary process. Metadata signals: folders “Legal”, “HR Legal”, “Disputes”, “Compliance”; filenames “claim”, “settlement”, “legal guidance”. Types of documents expected: legal dispute summaries; litigation registers; HR legal compliance memos; dismissal and disciplinary procedures; human rights policy; modern slavery statements (if applicable). Examples of document titles: “Legal Disputes Register”; “HR Legal Compliance Summary”; “Human Rights Policy”; “Dismissal Procedure”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Supplier labour audits → primary Supply Chain Management unless the main content is a broader human rights policy or significant downstream human rights incident.- Whistleblowing processes → Grievance Mechanisms and Monitoring primary. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: dispute/claim status statement OR documented dismissal/disciplinary procedure.Strong evidence: multi-year dispute register; outcomes and reserves; documented process controls; jurisdictional risk assessment where the Target operates in higher-risk regions. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): disputes/claims (status, outcomes); key jurisdictions and risk profile; dismissal process and controls; human rights commitments and due diligence approach (as relevant). Brief Finding Description (b): describe whether claims exist, whether the Target is exposed to higher-risk jurisdictions, and whether dismissal procedures reduce future dispute risk. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: formalise dismissal/disciplinary controls; close gaps in legal compliance process.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: build a simple human rights due diligence narrative aligned to UNGP/OECD expectations if value chain exposure is material. [11] Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if repeated disputes, significant settlements, or weak procedures exist.- Effort Medium if processes require redesign and rollout.Red flags examples: repeated dismissal claims; unresolved disputes; lack of documented disciplinary procedure; operating in higher-risk geographies with no due diligence narrative. Open questions/data requests: full dispute register (3–5 years); dismissal procedure evidence; reserves/provisions; union/works council involvement; human rights policy and due diligence steps, if applicable. Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: Labour and human rights risk appears low based on operating geography and limited dispute history; however, dismissal procedures should be clearly documented to reduce the risk of future employee complaints. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Ensure a clear and consistently applied dismissal procedure, including documentation and escalation requirements.'),
    (10, 'Grievance Mechanisms and Monitoring', 'ScopeReview of grievance procedure and the availability of reporting channels, as well as overview of reported cases of the last three years. Operational definitionThis category covers employee grievance handling and monitoring: whistleblowing/speak-up channels, anonymity, investigations, case tracking, anti-retaliation, and (where relevant) customer complaint governance when it is clearly a grievance mechanism rather than a service KPI. Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): whistleblowing, speak up, hotline, grievance, anonymous reporting, investigation process, retaliation, case log, ethics line, escalation, complaint channel (employee), disciplinary investigation. Metadata signals: folders “Compliance”, “Ethics”, “HR”; filenames “whistleblowing”, “hotline”. Types of documents expected: whistleblowing policy; hotline vendor contracts; investigation SOP; case logs; onboarding materials; grievance policy and monitoring metrics. Examples of document titles: “Whistleblowing Policy”; “Speak-Up Hotline Procedure”; “Investigation SOP”; “Grievance Case Log (Sanitised)”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Customer complaints about delivery/service → Customer Engagement unless the document is a governance procedure for grievances (e.g., serious complaint escalation, ethics-related grievances). Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: whistleblowing/grievance policy with channel description.Strong evidence: case logs (sanitised); investigation timelines; governance oversight; trend analysis. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): channels (internal vs third-party); anonymity options; investigation governance; evidence of monitoring/logging; case themes (if available). Brief Finding Description (b): describe whether channels exist, are accessible, and whether monitoring evidence exists (or is missing). Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: implement or strengthen independent channel and monitoring if absent.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: trend analysis to identify systemic root causes (culture, safety, HR process gaps). Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if no grievance mechanism exists (particularly for regulated or multi-site operations).- Effort Low–Medium for policy/process enhancements; Medium if case management tooling is required. Red flags examples: no anonymous option; no investigation process; no tracking/logging; retaliation risks not addressed. Open questions/data requests: case log (3 years); investigation SOP; evidence of non-retaliation controls; third-party hotline options; governance oversight evidence. Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: A whistleblowing policy exists and provides multiple reporting routes (including anonymous written reporting); however, monitoring evidence and case-tracking detail are limited in the reviewed materials. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: If not already in place, consider implementing a third-party whistleblowing channel and centralised case tracking.'),
    (11, 'Working Conditions & Terms of Employment', 'ScopeHigh-level review of contracts and systems in place (e.g., worker council, benefits, health insurance). Operational definitionThis category covers employment terms and compliance architecture: contract templates, employee categories, working time, benefits, pensions, profit-sharing, collective bargaining agreements (CBAs), and works council arrangements. Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): employment contract, contract template, collective bargaining agreement, CBA, works council, pension contributions, benefits, health insurance, working hours, overtime, profit-sharing, termination terms. Metadata signals: folders “HR”, “People”, “Contracts”, “Benefits”; filenames “contract template”, “CBA”. Types of documents expected: sample contracts/templates; benefits summaries; pension scheme documents; CBA summaries; working time policies; termination terms. Examples of document titles: “Standard Employment Contract Template”; “Benefits Guide”; “CBA Summary”; “Pension Scheme Overview”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Dismissal litigation → Labour Laws & Human Rights primary.- Engagement surveys → Employee Engagement primary. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: contract template or CBA/benefits overview.Strong evidence: documented analysis of CBA alignment; quantification of potential liabilities; clear mapping of employee categories to applicable terms. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): employee categories and contract types; CBA coverage; benefits and pensions; flagged non-alignment risks; dependency on legal workstream for interpretation. Brief Finding Description (b): describe the employment architecture and any flagged potential misalignment (especially where legal guidance indicates risk). Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: quantify and remediate CBA/pension misalignment; confirm treatment of profit-sharing.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: standardise documentation and approvals; improve evidencing for future HR compliance. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if widespread benefit/pension underpayment is plausible.- Effort Medium–High if remediation requires policy/contract changes across large headcount. Red flags examples: legal guidance flags misalignment; inconsistent contract templates; undocumented pension/profit-sharing treatment. Open questions/data requests: employee headcount by category; CBA applicability mapping; pension/profit-sharing treatment evidence; quantified exposure; any remediation in progress. Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: Standard contract templates exist, and employee categories align to defined terms; however, legal guidance flags a potential collective bargaining/pension alignment issue (including profit-sharing treatment) that requires legal workstream assessment and potential remediation. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: Address potential CBA non-alignment in coordination with legal DD; quantify exposure and remediation options.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: None identified.'),
    (12, 'Employee Engagement', 'ScopeAssessment of systems and management regarding employee surveys, benefits, talent retention, and career development. Operational definitionThis category covers how the Target measures and manages employee engagement and retention: surveys, training and onboarding, benefits and wellbeing programmes, turnover/retention reporting, and action plans. Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): employee survey, engagement score, retention, turnover, voluntary turnover, involuntary turnover, training completion, onboarding, benefits, wellbeing, talent development, career development. Metadata signals: folders “HR”, “People”, “Survey”, “Training”; filenames “survey”, “turnover”. Types of documents expected: engagement survey outputs; HR dashboards; training matrices; onboarding packs; benefits overviews; termination summaries; career development frameworks. Examples of document titles: “Employee Engagement Survey Results”; “Training Completion Dashboard”; “Benefits Overview”; “Staff Turnover Summary”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Safety training with incident prevention purpose → Health and Safety Management category.- DEI training and harassment policies → Diversity & Inclusion. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: survey output or benefits overview.Strong evidence: turnover KPIs (voluntary/involuntary) over time; survey coverage includes operational sites; documented action plan and follow-up. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): survey mechanism and coverage; satisfaction/engagement themes; turnover metrics (or gaps); training completion; benefits; evidence of follow-through actions. Brief Finding Description (b): describe whether engagement is measured across the workforce and whether retention is tracked; highlight gaps (missing turnover KPIs; surveys excluding operational sites). Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: address retention risks if turnover is high or engagement weak.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: collect turnover KPIs and extend surveys to operational/manufacturing sites; strengthen feedback loop. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if the Target has high turnover in critical roles and no retention plan.- Effort Low to implement KPIs; Medium if programmes must be redesigned. Red flags examples: no turnover KPIs; survey excludes operational sites; repeated negative themes without action plan. Open questions/data requests: turnover data last 3 years; survey instrument and raw outputs; action plan evidence; training logs for key programmes. Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: Benefits and employee surveys exist; however, turnover KPIs are not consistently tracked and survey coverage appears to exclude parts of the operational workforce, reducing insight into retention and engagement at production sites.Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Track voluntary/involuntary turnover KPIs and ensure surveys include operational/manufacturing sites; document action plans and follow-up.'),
    (13, 'Diversity & Inclusion', 'ScopeHigh-level review of DEI policy/procedures, pay gap reporting, and cases of harassment/discrimination. Operational definitionThis category covers equal opportunity and inclusion: DEI policies, anti-harassment and anti-discrimination controls, workforce composition metrics (including leadership representation), targets, pay equity/pay gap monitoring, and incident handling. Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): diversity, inclusion, equality, discrimination, harassment, equal pay, pay gap, gender representation, leadership diversity, DEI targets, inclusive workplace, zero tolerance.Metadata signals: folders “HR”, “DEI”, “Inclusion”; filenames “equality policy”, “gender pay gap”. Types of documents expected: DEI policies; workforce demographic summaries; board/management composition; pay gap analyses; anti-harassment training; incident summaries. Examples of document titles: “Diversity & Equality Policy”; “Workforce Gender Breakdown”; “Pay Gap Analysis”; “Anti-Harassment Training Completion”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Whistleblowing and grievance tools → Grievance Mechanisms and Monitoring primary unless DEI violations dominate the document’s purpose. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: DEI policy or demographic metrics.Strong evidence: targets + action plan; pay equity monitoring; incident trend evidence and controls. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): policy coverage; representation by level; targets; pay gap reporting; harassment/discrimination case handling (if available). Brief Finding Description (b): describe whether DEI is governed beyond policy—i.e., targets, monitoring, and transparency. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: strengthen harassment controls if evidence suggests risk.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: set measurable representation targets and consider pay equity/pay gap monitoring disclosures. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if harassment/discrimination incidents exist without controls or if legal exposure is plausible.- Effort Low–Medium for targets/monitoring; higher if structural changes are required. Red flags examples: no targets; no pay gap reporting; no training evidence; lack of incident governance. Open questions/data requests: demographic breakdown by grade; target-setting documents; pay gap analysis; training evidence; incident summary (sanitised). Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: A diversity and equality policy is in place, and gender representation is tracked; however, no targets are evidenced for improving management representation and pay gap reporting is not provided. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Establish measurable DEI targets and consider publishing/pay equity monitoring to demonstrate maturity and reduce reputational risk.'),
    (14, 'Stakeholder Engagement', 'ScopeHigh-level review of procedures and management relating to stakeholder engagement, community issues, and political parties. Operational definitionThis category covers engagement with stakeholder groups beyond customers and suppliers: community initiatives, donations/philanthropy governance, NGO relationships, public communications, and governance for political contributions or lobbying (even if “none”). Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): stakeholder mapping, community engagement, donations, philanthropy, sponsorship, NGO engagement, volunteering, public communications, political contributions, lobbying, stakeholder dialogue, community investment. Metadata signals: folders “CSR”, “Community”, “Stakeholders”; filenames “donations policy”, “community”. Types of documents expected: stakeholder maps; community investment reports; donations and sponsorship policies; political contributions statements; NGO engagement notes. Examples of document titles: “Stakeholder Mapping”; “Community Initiatives Overview”; “Donations Policy”; “Political Activity Statement”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Materiality assessment stakeholder input → ESG Strategy primary, Stakeholder Engagement secondary. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: stakeholder group list/map or community engagement overview.Strong evidence: donations governance (approval thresholds); documented policy statement on political engagement; KPI tracking. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): stakeholder groups; engagement mechanisms; community initiatives; donations/political activity governance; reputational considerations. Brief Finding Description (b): describe stakeholder groups and whether political engagement is ruled out or governed. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: confirm political engagement is absent and maintain governance.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: implement clear policy and governance for donations and political contributions; track engagement outcomes. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if donations/political activity occurs without governance.- Effort Low to formalise policy/governance. Red flags examples: donations without approvals; informal political involvement; reputational controversies without response plan. Open questions/data requests: donations register; sponsorship list; political activity statement; stakeholder engagement plan; comms governance. Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: Stakeholder groups are identified and community engagement initiatives exist; political engagement is described as low/no material and not evidenced in the reviewed materials. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: Confirm and maintain that the Target is not engaged in political activities.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Develop a clear policy and governance structure around donations and political contributions.'),
    (15, 'Energy Efficiency', 'ScopeReview of energy efficiency programmes and established energy costs and the potential value at stake from improved efficiency. Operational definitionThis category covers energy consumption and efficiency management: audits/surveys, tracking by site/entity, compliance with audit requirements where applicable, implemented measures, and maturity of energy management systems. ISO 50001 provides a commonly used framework for an energy management system. [12] Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): energy audit, energy survey, electricity consumption, kWh, MWh, energy intensity, district heating, metering, HVAC, LED retrofit, energy efficiency measures, ISO 50001. Metadata signals: folders “Energy”, “Utilities”, “Facilities”; filenames “energy audit”, “consumption”, “MWh”. Types of documents expected: energy audit reports; utility data exports; site energy trackers; energy reduction project lists; ISO 50001 documents; evidence of renewable electricity procurement. Examples of document titles: “Energy Audit Report”; “Electricity Consumption 2022–2024”; “Energy Efficiency Measures Tracker”; “ISO 50001 Readiness”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- GHG inventory documents (Scopes, tCO₂e) → GHG Emissions & Carbon Footprint primary; Energy Efficiency secondary if energy data is a core driver.- Renewable electricity certificates with assurance focus → consider Risk Management & Assurance secondary. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: at least one energy consumption dataset or audit report covering main sites/entities.Strong evidence: site/entity coverage across footprint; audit cadence + compliance evidence; quantified savings; owners and timing for measures. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): consumption by site and year; energy sources and major drivers; audit timing and compliance evidence; implemented measures; coverage gaps (e.g., manufacturing entities missing). Brief Finding Description (b): describe (i) whether energy audits are performed, (ii) how energy is tracked, (iii) where data gaps exist, and (iv) evidence of implemented measures. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: maintain audit compliance and evidence retention; close gaps for excluded entities.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: implement systematic energy management (ISO 50001-style) to reduce costs and strengthen continuous improvement. [13] Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if audit compliance is missed and legal enforcement or customer requirements are likely.- Effort Low–Medium for audits and data consolidation; Medium–High if metering upgrades are required. Red flags examples: no energy data for material sites; overdue audits; unsubstantiated “100% renewable” claims without evidence trail. Open questions/data requests: full site/entity energy dataset; audit schedule and reports; renewable procurement evidence; energy reduction business cases; metering plan. Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: Energy audits have been conducted and energy reduction measures implemented; electricity consumption is tracked for core locations, but energy data for certain manufacturing entities is missing and the exact electricity source mix is not disclosed. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: Ensure energy audits are conducted on time and evidence is maintained to avoid compliance risk.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Consider ISO 50001-style energy management and complete energy data coverage across the full operating footprint. [13]'),
    (16, 'GHG Emissions & Carbon Footprint', 'ScopeReview of management of GHG emissions including allocation and review of any carbon footprint assessment by the Target. Operational definitionThis category covers GHG inventorying (Scopes 1–3 or equivalent), targets and transition planning, boundary/methodology, drivers of emissions, and completeness of emissions data. IFRS S2 requires disclosure of climate-related risks and opportunities, including climate-related physical risks and emissions-related disclosures, forming a useful reference structure where adopted. [14] Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): GHG inventory, carbon footprint, Scope 1, Scope 2, Scope 3, tCO₂e, emissions factor, baseline year, net zero, decarbonisation, SBTi, transition plan. Metadata signals: folders “GHG”, “Carbon”, “Climate”; filenames “Scope 3”, “inventory”, “tCO2e”. Types of documents expected: GHG workbooks; consultant footprint reports; target submissions; emissions dashboards; fleet fuel logs; supplier engagement plans for Scope 3. Examples of document titles: “Scope 1–3 Inventory FY2024”; “Carbon Footprint Methodology Memo”; “SBTi Target Submission”; “Fleet Emissions Dashboard”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Physical risk assessments → Natural Hazards & Climate Change Risk primary.- Energy efficiency audit reports → Energy Efficiency primary unless the focus is tCO₂e inventory/targets. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: at least one of (inventory outputs; calculation workbook; methodology note; target statement with defined boundary).Strong evidence: Scopes clearly defined; baseline year and target timelines; independent review/assurance; documented assumptions and data source traceability. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): boundary and Scopes; baseline year; targets and interim milestones; main emission drivers; methodology and assumptions; data gaps; decarbonisation levers. Brief Finding Description (b): describe (i) what Scopes are covered, (ii) whether targets are credible vs evidence, (iii) whether the latest inventory is available, and (iv) completeness across footprint. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: address credibility/data completeness gaps if disclosures or customer demands rely on them.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: review updated inventory assumptions once available; build transition plan with prioritized levers and governance. [15] Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if the Target has major customer/regulatory expectations for emissions disclosure but cannot evidence Scopes/boundary.- Effort Medium–High if building Scope 3 and supplier data collection is required. Red flags examples: targets without baseline; major Scope exclusions without rationale; large year-on-year changes unexplained. Open questions/data requests: latest inventory outputs; boundary statement; emissions factors and methodology; Scope 3 supplier coverage; abatement levers and capex dependencies. Sample filled exampleBrief Finding Description: The Target has initiated value-chain GHG calculations and target-setting; however, the latest consolidated results were not available for review at the time of assessment and full footprint data coverage should be confirmed to support credible tracking. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Review consolidated Scope 1–3 outputs once available, including assumptions and inputs, and complete data coverage across all entities to support transition planning. [15]'),
    (17, 'Natural Hazards & Climate Change Risk', 'ScopeReview of the Target’s management of current risks posed by natural hazards (floods, earthquakes, wildfires, etc.) and future risks posed by climate change. Operational definitionThis category covers exposure to physical climate hazards and natural hazards (current and future): site vulnerability, business interruption, insurance implications, and adaptation/resilience planning. TCFD recommendations provide a widely used structure for identifying and disclosing climate-related risks (including physical risks) and scenario considerations. [16] Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): climate physical risk, natural hazard, flood, heatwave, storm, wildfire, drought, sea level, resilience, adaptation, hazard map, business continuity. Metadata signals: folder names “Climate”, “Risk”, “Insurance”, “Business Continuity”; file names containing “climate risk”, “flood”, “heat”, “storm”, “resilience”, “scenario”. Types of documents expected in this topic: climate/physical risk assessments; insurance risk engineering reports; site hazard screenings; resilience CAPEX plans; BCPs with climate hazard annexes; asset/location exposure maps. Examples of document titles: “Physical Climate Risk Screening – Sites”; “Flood Risk Assessment – Distribution Centre”; “Insurance Risk Survey – Property & BI”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- General BCP with no hazard detail → do not place here; only include if climate/natural hazards are explicit.- Climate transition plans (targets, abatement) → treat primarily under GHG Emissions & Carbon Footprint; tag here only where physical risk content is substantive. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: at least one site list + hazard assessment OR insurer/site risk note.Strong evidence: hazard severity by site; time horizon; quantified exposure (BI days/cost); documented action plan with owners and timing. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): sites covered; hazards assessed; methodology/time horizon; quantified impacts; mitigation/adaptation actions; gaps. Brief Finding Description (b): describe (i) whether risks were assessed, (ii) whether specific sites are linked to hazards, (iii) whether an action plan exists, and (iv) any missing quantification. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: close critical gaps (e.g., unassessed sites, no plan for high-exposure assets).- Value Creation / Best-Practice: integrate into ERM/insurance renewal strategy and capex prioritisation. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if high-exposure sites exist with no mitigation and likely BI/liability implications.- Remediation effort: Medium–High if multi-site physical risk assessment + capex planning required. Red flags examples: repeated flood/heat shutdowns; insurer exclusions; “no site-specific assessment available”. Open questions/data requests: site register + addresses; insurance claims history; resilience capex plan; disaster recovery tests/drills. Sample filled example (end-report-aligned style)Brief Finding Description: Physical climate risks have been considered at a high level; however, the materials reviewed do not specify site-level exposure, quantified impacts, or a documented action plan to address climate-related physical risks. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: None identified.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Complete a site-specific physical risk screening and document a resilience action plan aligned to priority sites, incorporating insurance and business continuity considerations. [17]'),
    (18, 'Environmental Management', 'ScopeSummarises findings across environmental topics within the EHS section, typically including: environmental management structure, budgets/capex/provisions, audits and inspections, permitting, enforcement actions, restricted substances (including asbestos/PCB/PFAS), and soil/groundwater impacts. Operational definitionThis category covers the Target’s environmental compliance and environmental management maturity, including permitting and legal obligations (e.g., industrial emissions permitting and major hazards regimes where applicable), environmental audits/inspections, legacy site liabilities (soil/groundwater), and hazardous/restricted substances management. EU chemicals compliance such as REACH places the burden of proof on companies to identify and manage chemical risks. [18] Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): environmental permit, ISO 14001, environmental audit, regulator inspection, contaminated land, Phase I assessment, soil and groundwater, hazardous substances, restricted substances, REACH, PFAS, asbestos register. Metadata signals: folders “EHS”, “HSE”, “Environment”, “Permits”, “Waste”, “Chemicals”; file names containing “permit”, “environmental code”, “Phase I”, “soil”, “PFAS”, “asbestos”. Types of documents expected in this topic: permit registers; regulator correspondence; environmental audit reports; ISO 14001 related materials and certificates; contaminated site register extracts; Phase I/II studies; asbestos/PCB/PFAS inventories; decommissioning documentation; waste/chemicals registers. Examples of document titles: “Environmental Permit Register”; “Authority Inspection Letter”; “Site Contamination Screening”; “PFAS Inventory and Substitution Plan”; “ISO 14001 Certificate”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Product/packaging compliance memos → Sustainable Products primary unless they directly relate to site waste handling/permitting.- Occupational safety incidents and workplace injuries → Health and Safety Management primary. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: permit overview OR environmental audit/inspection evidence OR contaminated site register evidence.Strong evidence: site-by-site permitting status; closed/open compliance actions; documented restricted substances inventory; soil/groundwater investigation reports and liability framing; evidence of decommissioning closure. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): permitting requirements and compliance status; environmental management system maturity and scope; audit and inspection outcomes; enforcement actions; restricted substances exposure and substitution plans; soil/groundwater risks and evidence quality. Brief Finding Description (b): describe (i) whether environmental obligations are controlled with evidence, (ii) whether there are legacy liabilities, (iii) status of any decommissioning/environmental closure actions, and (iv) key data gaps (missing reports, incomplete registers). Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: investigate suspected soil/groundwater impacts; close decommissioning obligations; complete restricted substances inventories and substitution (PFAS where relevant). - Value Creation / Best-Practice: strengthen evidence retention and consistency across sites; expand EMS coverage where it materially improves control. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if contamination is plausible and remediation could exceed material thresholds, or if permits/enforcement actions indicate systemic non-compliance.- Remediation effort Medium–High where investigations/remediation are required across multiple sites. Red flags examples: sites listed as potentially contaminated without investigation; open regulator actions; PFAS presence without inventory/substitution plan; missing permit documentation. Open questions/data requests: permit register by site; inspection reports and closure evidence; contaminated site register extracts; Phase I/II studies; asbestos/PCB/PFAS inventories; decommissioning records. Sample filled example (end-report-aligned style)Brief Finding Description: Environmental compliance appears generally controlled; however, one or more sites are recorded as potentially contaminated and supporting investigation reports are incomplete. Restricted substances risks are acknowledged, and additional inventory and substitution actions (e.g., PFAS-containing materials) may be required. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: Conduct/complete soil and groundwater investigations at historically impacted sites and implement PFAS inventory/substitution where relevant.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Strengthen group-wide evidence retention for permitting, inspections, and environmental closure actions to support transaction assurance.'),
    (19, 'Health and Safety Management', 'ScopeSummarises findings across health & safety topics within the EHS section, typically including: H&S management structure, budgets/capex, audits and inspections, claims/litigation/enforcement actions, safety culture and performance (accident investigation, training/PPE), workplace risk assessments and exposure management, and emergency/fire/explosion protection. Operational definitionThis category covers occupational health & safety (OH&S) management maturity, performance, incident and claims exposure, compliance with workplace safety expectations, and emergency preparedness. ISO 45001 provides an internationally recognised OH&S management system framework and is often used as a maturity reference in DD. [22] Categorisation instructionsPrimary routing keywords (8–12): accident, injury, incident report, near miss, workplace risk assessment, PPE, safety training, regulator notice, machine guarding, work instructions, evacuation drill, emergency plan, ISO 45001. Metadata signals: folders “Safety”, “H&S”, “Incidents”, “Risk assessments”, “Emergency”; filenames “incident log”, “training”, “inspection notice”. Types of documents expected in this topic: incident/near-miss logs; regulator correspondence; risk assessments; work instructions; safety training matrices; machinery inspection reports; claims registers; emergency and fire safety plans; drill protocols. Examples of document titles: “Accident Statistics 2022–2025”; “Workplace Risk Assessments”; “Regulator Inspection Letter”; “Emergency Evacuation Plan”; “Machine Inspection Report”. Edge-case rules and multi-tagging:- Environmental spills/contamination → Environmental Management primary unless harm to people is dominant.- Whistleblowing channels → Grievance Mechanisms primary. Evidence checklistMinimum evidence to avoid “Data gap”: H&S policy or incident statistics or risk assessments.Strong evidence: regulator correspondence and closure; multi-year accident trends; consistent site-level risk assessments; evidence of training/PPE; emergency drills with follow-up. Analysis instructionsKey extractions (a): accident/incident rates and trends; outstanding claims/enforcement actions; risk assessment coverage; machinery safety controls; training/PPE compliance evidence; emergency preparedness and drill frequency. Brief Finding Description (b): describe (i) H&S performance signals, (ii) whether risk assessments and work instructions are consistently in place, (iii) enforcement/claims exposure, and (iv) whether emergency preparedness is evidenced at all sites. Opportunities (c):- Risk Related Measures: standardise and complete workplace risk assessments and work instructions; ensure machine safety measures are functional and verified; close any enforcement actions.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: strengthen management system consistency and documentation across all production sites; consider ISO 45001 alignment/certification where proportionate. Ratings, materiality, effort (d):- Potential major risk if there is an ongoing serious injury investigation, open enforcement action, or repeated accidents indicating systemic control failure.- Remediation effort Medium–High if work instructions/risk assessments require standardisation across multiple sites and machinery types. Red flags examples: open regulator investigations; repeated machinery incidents; lack of risk assessments; inconsistent training evidence; emergency plans missing at some sites. Open questions/data requests: complete incident log (3–5 years); regulator enforcement status; machinery safety inspection schedule; site risk assessment register; training completion logs; emergency plan and drill evidence. Sample filled example (end-report-aligned style)Brief Finding Description: H&S management structures and internal inspections are in place, and emergency preparedness evidence exists at multiple sites; however, risk assessments and work instructions are not consistently evidenced across all work tasks and machinery, and an ongoing injury-related investigation indicates residual liability and control risk. Opportunity for Improvement:- Risk Related Measures: Ensure risk assessments and work instructions are completed and consistently implemented across all production sites and machinery; reinforce follow-up of machine safety controls.- Value Creation / Best-Practice: Strengthen group-wide documentation and audit readiness, aligning to recognised OH&S management system practices where proportionate.')
